# ADR: Optional typed parent-shell integration

## Status
Accepted

## Context
A normal uhm child cannot persist cd, environment, or source effects in its caller. Returning success from the child would be false, while arbitrary generated shell source evaluated by a wrapper would create an unnecessarily broad execution boundary.

## Decision
Offer an explicitly installed, static Bash/Zsh/Fish wrapper. The model may propose exactly one typed change-directory, set-environment, unset-environment, or source-file action. A nonce-bound owner-only control directory transports the action through fixed filenames; the binary opens control paths without following links and performs fixed-name operations relative to a validated directory descriptor. Only local audited renderers produce shell builtins. Parent outcome is unknown until a matching wrapper acknowledgement updates the queued enum-only telemetry candidate and optional local history.

## Alternatives Considered
- Print commands only: truthful but does not deliver the requested parent-shell result.
- Evaluate model-written shell source: more flexible but violates the narrow typed boundary and compound-action limit.
- Prompt hooks or a resident agent: broader context but conflicts with invocation-only operation and the product boundary.
- Universal undo: unsupported because sourced code and arbitrary shell state are not transactionally reversible.

## Consequences
The integration is optional, auditable, removable, and keeps normal stdout/stderr byte-clean. It supports only four single actions and three shells. Sourced code retains full user authority and can terminate the shell before acknowledgement or cleanup, so those outcomes remain honestly unknown.
